**1. A method of controlling exhaust gas recirculation in a turbocharged internal combustion engine having a compressor, a turbine, and a low-pressure exhaust gas recirculation passage extending from an exhaust passage downstream of the turbine to an intake passage upstream of the compressor, the method comprising:**

receiving a commanded change from a first target exhaust gas recirculation ratio to a second target exhaust gas recirculation ratio;

estimating, during the commanded change, an in-cylinder exhaust gas recirculation ratio based on an exhaust-gas transport delay through the low-pressure exhaust gas recirculation passage and the intake passage;

determining a transient deviation between the estimated in-cylinder exhaust gas recirculation ratio and the second target exhaust gas recirculation ratio;

controlling, based on the transient deviation, a low-pressure exhaust gas recirculation valve and at least one of an intake throttle, a compressor bypass valve, a turbine wastegate, a variable-geometry turbine, an ignition timing, a fuel-injection timing, a fuel-injection quantity, or an air-fuel ratio;

predicting an abnormal-combustion parameter based at least on the estimated in-cylinder exhaust gas recirculation ratio and an engine operating condition; and

while the estimated in-cylinder exhaust gas recirculation ratio transitions toward the second target exhaust gas recirculation ratio, limiting a rate or magnitude of the commanded change and adjusting the at least one of the ignition timing, the fuel-injection timing, the fuel-injection quantity, or the air-fuel ratio to maintain the predicted abnormal-combustion parameter below a threshold indicative of knock, pre-ignition, or misfire.

1. **A method of controlling an internal combustion engine** having an intake passage, an exhaust passage, a turbocharger including a compressor disposed in the intake passage, and an exhaust-gas-recirculation passage connecting the exhaust passage to the intake passage at a location upstream of the compressor, the method comprising:  

   determining a target exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio based on an operating condition of the internal combustion engine;  

   determining a current exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio;  

   determining a limit on a rate of change of the current exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio based on a condition associated with occurrence of abnormal combustion;  

   generating a commanded exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio by limiting a change from the current exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio toward the target exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio to the determined limit; and  

   controlling an amount of exhaust gas flowing through the exhaust-gas-recirculation passage such that the current exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio approaches the commanded exhaust-gas-recirculation ratio, thereby suppressing the abnormal combustion.
